course: dictionary
lesson: 4314
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word gross is a good example. «Ew, that's gross!» means disgusting, the way a slimy or smelly thing is. The clue is the Ew, which shows a face-scrunching reaction.
explain: In maths, gross can mean total, counting everything together. «The gross weight of the box is 2 kilograms» includes the goods and the packaging. The clue is weight and box, so the word is about adding everything up, not about being disgusting.
explain: Gross can also be a number. A gross is 144, which is twelve dozen. «The shop sold a gross of pencils.» The word «of» and the idea of a counting group tell you this is the number meaning, like a dozen.
explain: Read the whole sentence and try each meaning in place of the hard word. Whichever meaning still makes sense is the right one.
fact: Gross has several meanings, and the context tells you which one is meant.
fact: Informal: «Ew, that's gross!» means disgusting or very unpleasant.
fact: Total: gross pay is the big number before tax; net pay is what is left after tax.
fact: A gross is 144, which is twelve dozen (12 × 12).
fact: Formal: «a gross mistake» means very bad and obvious. As a verb, to gross means to earn a total amount of money.
exercise: «Ew, that's gross!» What does gross mean here? | Total, before anything is taken away | A group of 144 | Very bad and obvious | *Disgusting
exercise: «Gross pay is the big number at the top of the payslip.» What does gross mean in this sentence? | Disgusting | A group of 144 | *Total, before anything is taken away | Very bad and obvious
exercise: How many are in a gross? | 24 | 12 | 100 | *144
test: «It was a gross mistake to forget the test.» What does gross mean here? | Disgusting | *Very bad and obvious | A group of 144 | Total, before anything is taken away
test: «The film grossed millions in its first week.» What does grossed mean? | *Earned a total amount of money | Smelled very bad | Lost everything | Counted in twelves
exam: Which meaning fits best in «The gross weight of the parcel includes the box.»? | *Total, counting everything together | Very bad and obvious | Twelve of something | Disgusting
